<header><p class="st-kicker">princely state · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Wankaner State</h1><p class="st-meta">1605 CE – 1948 CE (approx.) · capital Wankaner</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Jhala Rajput state of Kathiawar on the Machhu river, known for its palaces and for rulers prominent in Indian public life.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Wankaner was founded in 1605 by Sartanji, a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jhala">Jhala</a> ruling house of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/halvad">Halvad</a> (later <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/dhrangadhra-state">Dhrangadhra State</a>), who took lands on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/machchhu-river">Machhu</a> river in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathiawar">Kathiawar</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-raj">Raj</a> Sahebs ruled a territory of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> and grain villages, paying tribute to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaekwads">Gaekwads</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/junagadh-state">Junagadh</a> before British protection after 1807. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amarsinhji-of-wankaner">Amarsinhji</a>, who reigned from 1881 to 1948, built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-vilas-palace">Ranjit Vilas Palace</a> and modernised the administration. It was an eleven-gun salute state.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Wankaner merged into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/saurashtra-state">United State of Saurashtra</a> in 1948 and became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> in 1960. Its royal family remained active in politics, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/digvijaysinhji">Digvijaysinhji</a> served as a Union minister.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/wankaner-state.jpg" alt="Wankaner State" width="320" height="208"><figcaption>Image: Xufanc · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Wankaner_State.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1605 CE – 1948 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>princely state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Wankaner</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Sartanji, Amarsinhji</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Gujarati</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Wankaner</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1776&amp;focus=wankaner-state" aria-label="Wankaner State on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6578 1343 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Wankaner State lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6898 1599L7364 1601M6898 1599L7364 1602M6898 1599L6896 1610"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7364 1601h0M7364 1602h0M6896 1610h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6898 1599h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6898 1599h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Wankaner. <a href="/atlas?year=1776&amp;focus=wankaner-state">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
